# mmcalc settings for the ac9s example.
#
# 1. The mathematical context: the target database.
# 2. The parsing rules.  To parse the expression ` x y z ` we construct
#    the unique proof tree of ` TOPLEVEL x y z `; that proof tree *is*
#    the parse tree of ` x y z `.  The TOPLEVEL token and its axioms are
#    provided here, so the mmcalc code itself never knows any specific
#    Metamath token (no `(`, `)`, `|-`, `<->`, ...).
#
# The TOP.* axioms below mimic the grammar nonterminals.  Their
# conclusions are ` TOPLEVEL ph `, ` TOPLEVEL x `, ` TOPLEVEL A `,
# ` TOPLEVEL |- ph `, using set.mm's own variables (`$f wff ph $`,
# `$f setvar x $`, `$f class A $` are all in scope at the end of set.mm's
# $v/$f declarations), so MMA can unify any composite expression into the
# variable and `improve all` builds the whole grammar tree underneath.

$[ set.mm $]

$c TOPLEVEL $.

TOP.wff $a TOPLEVEL ph $.
TOP.set $a TOPLEVEL x $.
TOP.class $a TOPLEVEL A $.
TOP.turnstile $a TOPLEVEL |- ph $.